Title: The Woman at the Window
Order: 3
Production Code: 1x03

Cast: JIM DAVIS, JULIA, THE NARRATOR. Running time target: 13-16 minutes.

PRODUCTION NOTE: The first living conversation in the series. Everything said before this is Jim talking to himself; from here on a second voice answers. Mix Julia very close and a little reverberant in her own house (silk, wood, plaster), then dry and open in the car.

EXT. FIFTH AVENUE - NEAR 72ND STREET - CONTINUOUS

AMB: the empty avenue, the Ford's engine idling, a wind that lifts a paper and drops it

SFX: the Ford wheeling in a sudden circle; tires shrieking on stone

SFX: a dull, soft thump under a wheel - a child's still body - then the car leaping up onto the curb

Jim does not react to the thump with speech. A breath goes out of him. Then the engine cuts.

SFX: the engine dies; a tick of cooling metal

NARRATOR
He wheeled the car in a sudden circle, running over the still body of a child and leaping on the curb. Then he rushed up the steps, and tried the door, and rang violently.

SFX: running steps on stone; a heavy door; a doorbell, long, clanging, the sound of a bell that rings too far into the house

SFX: Jim rattling the door; leaning on the bell

JIM DAVIS
(pounding, calling)
Hello! Open the door! Hello!

AMB: the house inside - a distant clock; a long, heavy stillness

A long pause. Hold it.

SFX: footsteps coming from far inside, fast, unsteady; a key; a chain; a bolt

SFX: the heavy door swinging back

INT. JULIA'S HOUSE - THE FRONT HALL

AMB: a hall of marble and silk; a grandfather clock; the faintest hum of an electric light

They stare at each other in silence.

NARRATOR
They stared a moment in silence. She had not noticed before that he was a Negro. He had not thought of her as white.

MUSIC: BED IN - a single sustained chord on a cello, cold, open, the sound of two worlds that do not meet

JULIA
(breathless, brittle)
You -

NARRATOR
She was a woman of perhaps twenty-five - rarely beautiful and richly gowned, with darkly-golden hair, and jewels.

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(bitter)
Yesterday she would scarcely have looked at him twice. He would have been dirt beneath her silken feet.

NARRATOR
She stared at him. Of all the sorts of men she had pictured as coming to her rescue she had not dreamed of one like him. Not that he was not human; but he dwelt in a world so far from hers, so infinitely far, that he seldom even entered her thought.

SFX: silk rustling; a jeweled hand tightening on a doorframe

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Yet as she looked at him curiously, he seemed quite commonplace and usual. He was a tall, dark workingman of the better class, with a sensitive face trained to stolidity, and a poor man's clothes and hands.

MUSIC: BED OUT

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
His face was soft and slow and his manner at once cold and nervous, like fires long banked, but not out.

A beat. Then the dead world outside comes through the open door like a wind.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
So a moment each paused and gauged the other; then the thought of the dead world without rushed in, and they started toward each other.

SFX: two sets of steps meeting in the hall; a sudden clasp of hands, then a release, embarrassed

JULIA
(rapid, high)
What has happened? Tell me! Nothing stirs. All is silence!

SFX: silk skirts dragging; a hand on his sleeve

JULIA (CONT'D)
(crying)
I see the dead strewn before my window as winnowed by the breath of God - and see -

SFX: heavy silk hangings sweeping aside on a rod; a rattle of rings

INT. JULIA'S HOUSE - THE DINING ROOM

AMB: a long room of mahogany and silver; a clock, softer here; a clink of crystal as a chandelier moves

SFX: Julia's hand tight on Jim's arm; the tremor in her breath

NARRATOR
She dragged him through great silken hangings to where, beneath the sheen of mahogany and silver, a little French maid lay stretched in quiet, everlasting sleep, and near her a butler lay prone in his livery.

SFX: a bell-pull swinging softly; a tray on a sideboard shivering with a fork that rolls and stops

Her crying breaks. She clings.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
The tears streamed down the woman's cheeks, and she clung to his arm until the perfume of her breath swept his face and he felt the tremors racing through her body.

SFX: her breathing, close, fast, very near the microphone

JULIA
(shaking)
I had been shut up in my dark room developing pictures of the comet which I took last night. When I came out - I saw the dead!

SFX: she turns on him

JULIA (CONT'D)
(a cry)
What has happened?

SFX: Jim is slow to answer; a long breath out

JIM DAVIS
(slowly)
Something - comet or devil - swept across the earth this morning, and - many are dead.

JULIA
(a whisper)
Many? Very many?

JIM DAVIS
I have searched, and I have seen no other living soul but you.

MUSIC: STING - one low note, held, under the lack of an answer

She gasps. They stare at each other.

JULIA
(barely audible)
My - father!

JIM DAVIS
Where is he?

JULIA
He started for the office.

JIM DAVIS
Where is it?

JULIA
In the Metropolitan Tower.

JIM DAVIS
Leave a note for him here, and come.

SFX: his steps, to the door; then they stop

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(a different voice, firm)
No. First, we must go - to Harlem.

JULIA
(taken aback)
Harlem!

SFX: her foot tapping, quick, impatient, on the marble floor

A silence - her understanding arrives in sound: the tapping slows and stops.

NARRATOR
She tapped her foot at first impatiently. She looked back and shuddered. Then she came resolutely down the steps.

SFX: her steps on the stone steps outside; heels, a pause; her skirts swishing

JULIA
(back to practical)
There's a swifter car in the garage in the court.

JIM DAVIS
I don't know how to drive it.

JULIA
I do.

EXT. THE COURT - GARAGE

SFX: a heavy garage door sliding on its rail; a groan of iron

SFX: a motor's starter; a rich, large engine catches; a gunned throttle, an unmistakable roar

NARRATOR
In ten minutes they were flying to Harlem on the wind.

MUSIC: BED IN - a driving, urgent rhythm in low strings and a pulse on a muted drum; the engine and the music are the same sound

AMB: the open air, high speed - a hiss of tires, rushing wind

SFX: the Stutz rising and racing, a high singing whine, "like an airplane"

SFX: a hard turn on two wheels; tires screaming; a shriek of rubber

NARRATOR
The Stutz rose and raced like an airplane. They took the turn at 110th Street on two wheels, and slipped with a shriek into 135th.

SFX: the brakes; a skid; the engine idling

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. 135TH STREET, HARLEM

AMB: a street in Harlem at midday; no life in it; a window shade tapping on a sill

SFX: Jim's door; he gets out; steps, fast, into a building

SFX: Julia alone, engine ticking; a clock down the street; a silence she is afraid of

NARRATOR
He was gone but a moment.

SFX: his steps returning, slow; the car door; a long, rough breath

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Then he returned, and his face was gray. She did not look, but said:

JULIA
(gently)
You have lost - somebody?

JIM DAVIS
(simply)
I have lost - everybody.

A beat; then, quietly:

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
Unless -

SFX: he is out of the car and running; a door; steps on stairs; a long, long silence

NARRATOR
He ran back, and was gone several minutes - hours they seemed to her.

SFX: Julia's fingers drumming on the wheel; stopping; an intake of breath; the clock down the street ticks louder

SFX: footsteps returning, very slow

JIM DAVIS
(low)
Everybody.

NARRATOR
He walked slowly back with something film-like in his hand, which he stuffed into his pocket.

SFX: a small sound of cloth; the pocket flap; his hand pressing down on something

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(after a moment)
I'm afraid I was selfish.

SFX: the engine; the car moves

NARRATOR
But already the car was moving toward the park, among the dark and lined dead of Harlem. The brown, still faces. The knotted hands. The homely garments. And the silence - the wild and haunting silence.

AMB: wind in the park trees; a swing creaking on its chain; no children

SFX: the Stutz slowing, almost reverent, then pulling out

EXT. FIFTH AVENUE - HEADING SOUTH

SFX: the Stutz down Fifth Avenue, in and out among the dead, a smooth, quiet murmur

NARRATOR
Out of the park, and down Fifth Avenue they whirled. In and out among the dead they slipped and quivered, needing no sound of bell or horn.

SFX: a brake squeal; a swerve; a body's coat-tail brushing the fender, a sigh of cloth

JULIA
(under her breath)
Sorry. Sorry.

INT. THE METROPOLITAN TOWER - ELEVATOR

SFX: the car pulling up; doors; steps across a lobby of stone, hollow

NARRATOR
Until the great, square Metropolitan Tower hove in sight.

SFX: an elevator gate, folding, gently; a body lifted from the cab floor and laid aside, with the sound of a man trying not to hurt it

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Gently he laid the dead elevator boy aside. The car shot upward.

SFX: the elevator, rising; cables humming; a floor indicator clicking by each floor

MUSIC: BED IN - a low bass drone, rising a half-step with each floor

INT. THE METROPOLITAN TOWER - THE OFFICE CORRIDOR

AMB: a high building's wind in its walls

NARRATOR
The door of the office stood open. On the threshold lay the stenographer, and, staring at her, sat the dead clerk.

SFX: a paper moving on a draft; a chair's squeak as a body shifts

SFX: Julia stepping around the stenographer; her heel on the sill; a stifled sob

INT. J.B.H.'S PRIVATE OFFICE

SFX: a heavy inner door; a hush; a rolltop desk; a ticking mantel clock

NARRATOR
The inner office was empty. But a note lay on the desk, folded and addressed but unsent.

SFX: paper unfolding; Julia reading, silently, her breath catching

JULIA
(reading aloud, her voice shaking, then steadying)
"Dear Daughter: I've gone for a hundred mile spin in Fred's new Mercedes. Shall not be back before dinner. I'll bring Fred with me. J.B.H."

SFX: the paper falling to the desk

MUSIC: BED OUT

A long moment of room tone. Then, nervously:

JULIA (CONT'D)
(a gasp, then pulling herself together)
Come. We must search the city.

INT. THE STUTZ - THE SEARCH

MUSIC: BED IN - restless, repeating; a quick rhythm in the strings that never resolves

SFX: the Stutz, accelerating, then slowing, then turning; a montage of streets heard through an engine

NARRATOR
Up and down, over and across, back again, on went that ghostly search.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Everywhere was silence and death. Death and silence.

SFX: the engine echoing off tall buildings, then crossing a bridge - a hollow rumble of iron and a rhythmic thump of joints

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
They hunted from Madison Square to Spuyten Duyvel. They rushed across the Williamsburg Bridge. They swept over Brooklyn.

AMB: wind over water; gulls

SFX: the car stopping; Jim stands; a long quiet; the water lapping at a pier

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
From the Battery and Morningside Heights they scanned the river.

SFX: Julia, calling, her voice echoing back from stone: "Hello! Hello!" - then silence

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Silence, silence everywhere, and no human sign.

MUSIC: OUT - the strings stop mid-phrase

AMB: the empty city, wind, nothing
